 It is the smaller half of peer fetch and deliberately so. The asking half is
 the host's -- the compiler cannot fetch a peer itself (Library Rule 2 fixes
 `codex.foreword -> codex -> codex.os -> apps`, so citing the net stack from the
 compiler inverts the order), and the decision taken 2026-07-16 is that it never
 will: the host fetches and hands the work over in the `%%QUOTED-WORKS%%` blob
 the compiler already reads. That leaves this binary as the whole of the server
 side, and it needs to be nothing more than a socket around `serve-work`.

 The index is UNVERIFIED, and that is the honest choice rather than an omission.
 `repo-index-verified` filters a store down to the works whose author a manifest
 admits; this serves whatever is on its disk. A server has no business deciding
 what an asker may believe. The asker hashes what comes back and refuses it
 unless it addresses the hash asked for (`accept-reply`), and its import gate
 checks the signature, the pinned key and the trust floor. Serving an unadmitted
 work cannot hurt the asker, and refusing one here would only mean a peer's trust
 policy silently truncating somebody else's. Content addressing and trust are two
 checks; this binary owns neither.

 We say:

Section: Identity

  press : Keypair
  press = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-serve-tool"))

  serve-id : AgentIdentity
  serve-id = AgentIdentity { public-key = press.public-key }

Section: The Socket

 One connection at a time, the pattern codex-vm port forwarding expects: boot
 with `-portfwd <host>:9300` and the VM injects a SYN toward the guest listener
 for every host client that connects.

  serve-port : Integer = 9300

 The MAC now comes from the seam, which answers the bound card's own station
 address off RAL/RAH and falls back to the NE2000 constant when nothing is
 bound. That is the one of these four that had to move: a service sourcing
 frames from the emulator's address on real hardware has its replies dropped by
 the card's receive filter, which presents as a dead link rather than as a
 mismatch.

 The other three were codex-vm's NAT constants spelled here, and they are gone.
 `10.0.2.15` and `10.0.2.2` are the emulator's subnet, which nothing on a real
 LAN answers to, and the pre-seeded gateway MAC was the ARP resolution this
 machine is meant to perform. **The address is now acquired by DHCP**, which is
 how the box this serves from is configured (Damian, 2026-07-30). Under
 codex-vm the NAT answers with the same 10.0.2.15 it always assumed, so the
 emulated runs are unchanged in their values and changed in where they come
 from.

 A REFUSED LEASE IS NOT SERVED FROM. `dhcp-io-configure` answers a config whose
 state is not NetConfigured when nothing replies, and the entry point stops
 there rather than falling back to an address it invented: a service that
 transmits into a subnet it does not belong to is harder to diagnose at a
 sitting than one that says it has no address.

 The gateway MAC is no longer seeded into the ARP cache; it is RESOLVED. When
 this was written `net-process-arp` learned from any ARP frame that arrived,
 so filling the cache needed nothing but patience. That is cache poisoning by
 construction and the ruling of 2026-08-19 removed it, which turned the
 patience into a request: `serve-one` calls `net-io-resolve` for the gateway
 before it listens. Resolution still happens the way it will have to happen on
 the LAN, and now it happens the way a LAN host actually does it.

  fresh-transport : NetworkConfig -> TcpTransportState
  fresh-transport (cfg) =
    let sess = net-session-new (cfg.local-mac) (cfg.local-ip) (cfg.gateway) serve-port 0 (cfg.gateway) 1000
    in transport-new sess

Section: Answering

 `served` AND `short` ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS AND THE VERDICT NOW SAYS WHICH.
 The bare sender returned a transport and no count, so a reply that stopped
 part way was reported as `served` -- the one line an operator reads to decide
 whether a peer got its work. A requester that received half a framed reply
 sees a frame it cannot decode, and the server's own log agreed it was served.

 `net-io-accept`, `net-io-recv-loop`, `net-io-send` and `net-io-close` all carry
 Network rows, so every one of them is bound with `<-` inside an act and every
 function that reaches them declares the rows it inherits. The neighbouring
 server in `apps/ideas` let-binds these in pure signatures; that is the
 undeclared-effect-row breakage described above, not a pattern to copy.

  reply-with : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState, WorkRequestMsg -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  reply-with (ix) (ts) (req) =
    let reply = serve-work ix serve-id req
    in act
      s <- net-io-send-checked ts tag-work-reply (encode-work-reply-body reply)
      ts2 <- net-io-close (s.ns-transport)
      let verdict = if s.ns-complete == False then "short " else if reply-is-empty reply then "miss " else "served "
      in verdict & req.hash
    end

  answer : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState, AgentMessage -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  answer (ix) (ts) (m) =
    when m
      is MsgWorkRequest (req) -> reply-with ix ts req
      is otherwise -> "not-a-request"

  handle-conn : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  handle-conn (ix) (ts) = act
    r <- net-io-recv-loop ts 0
    if r.has-message == False then "no-message"
    else if r.tag /= tag-work-request then "not-a-request"
    else let dr = decode-agent-msg-checked (r.tag) (r.body)
    in if dr.valid == False then "malformed"
    else answer ix (r.transport) (dr.message)
  end

 THE GATEWAY IS ASKED FOR BY NAME NOW. `net-process-arp` learns only from a
 reply addressed to us since 2026-08-20 (Damian's ruling on cache poisoning),
 so the paragraph above no longer holds on its own: nothing else on the
 segment will teach us this mapping, and without the request `resolve-dst-mac`
 broadcasts every frame forever. It still works -- a switch delivers a
 broadcast -- and it is not what a host on a LAN does. This is the ask.

 It is inside the per-connection function rather than at the entry point
 because `fresh-transport` builds a NEW session for every connection and the
 cache goes with it, so a resolution done once at boot would be discarded by
 the second caller.

  serve-one : WorkIndex, NetworkConfig -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  serve-one (ix) (cfg) = act
    resolved <- net-io-resolve (fresh-transport cfg) (cfg.gateway)
    ts <- net-io-accept (net-io-listen resolved) 0
    when (ts.session).conn.state
      is TcpEstablished -> handle-conn ix ts
      is otherwise -> "no-conn"
  end

Section: Entry

 THE LEASE IS RENEWED BETWEEN CONNECTIONS, which is the only place this
 program is ever not blocked. `net-io-accept` gives up after fifty million
 empty polls and answers a transport that never established, so a quiet
 server comes back around here on its own and a busy one comes back after
 each answer. Both are the top of this loop.

 The clock is the HPET, read in seconds. It is a MONOTONIC count from the
 machine starting rather than a wall clock, and that is the right one:
 a lease is an interval, and a wall clock that a time sync steps forward
 would expire one early or hold one past its end.

  renew-fuel : Integer = 20000

  serve-loop : WorkIndex, NetworkConfig, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Integer
  serve-loop (ix) (cfg) (count) = act
    cfg2 <- dhcp-io-maybe-renew cfg (305419896 + count) renew-fuel hpet-seconds
    renewed <- report-renewal cfg cfg2
    diag <- serve-one ix cfg2
    print-line-uni ("serve " & show count & " " & diag)
    serve-loop ix cfg2 (count + 1)
  end

  report-renewal : NetworkConfig, NetworkConfig -> [Console.Write] Integer
  report-renewal (before) (after) =
    if before.lease-start == after.lease-start then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve: lease renewed, " & format-net-config after)
      let r = 0 in r
    end

 The bring-up runs BEFORE the index is built, so the card it reports is the one
 the socket will use. It is printed rather than assumed: every refusal path in
 the seam falls back to the NE2000 silently and by design, so a line naming the
 card that won is the only way an operator learns a real one was declined.

  serve-configured : WorkIndex, NetworkConfig -> [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing
  serve-configured (ix) (cfg) = act
    if net-is-configured cfg == False then print-line-uni "cdx-serve: no DHCP lease, nothing to serve from"
    else act
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve: " & format-net-config cfg & ", " & show (list-length (ix.wi-paths)) & " paths indexed, listening on guest port " & show serve-port)
      served <- serve-loop ix cfg 0
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve closed after " & show served)
    end
  end

  opening : [Device.Block, Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    card <- net-driver-bring-up
    let ix = repo-index-from-disk disk-load
    in act
      cfg <- dhcp-io-configure net-driver-mac "cdx-serve" 305419896 200000 hpet-seconds
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve: NIC " & net-driver-card-name card)
      serve-configured ix cfg
    end
  end
